body:
**Emulator versions:** 1.5.57, 1.5.58
**Platforms:** linux/arm64 (Docker Desktop on Apple Silicon), linux/amd64 (GitHub-hosted `ubuntu-latest` runner)
**Client:** `@google-cloud/spanner` 7.21.0, Node.js 22 and 26

### What happens

The repro creates a database, applies a schema through 479 schema-change calls (957 DDL statements), then drops the database, and repeats. After every drop the emulator has no databases left, yet its memory keeps rising in steps.

| Platform | Version | Memory after each drop (MiB) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| linux/arm64 | 1.5.57 | 799, 1538, 1545, 2252, 2256, 2254 |
| linux/arm64 | 1.5.58 | 800, 1540, 1546, 1547, 2261, 2245 |
| linux/amd64 | 1.5.57 | 758, 1497, 1502, 1505 |
| linux/amd64 | 1.5.58 | 763, 1177, 1179, 1181 |

Memory is container memory from `docker stats`, read after `db.delete()` resolves. The idle emulator starts at 12–53 MiB. In a separate run with a production-like schema, memory stayed flat over 30 idle seconds afterwards; removing the container was the only way we found to free it.

We also tried opening client sessions on the database (one insert and one query) and closing the database handle before dropping it (`DATA=1`, `CLOSE=1`; tested with `TABLES=130` on 1.5.58, linux/arm64). Neither changed the pattern.

### Why it matters

Test suites commonly create one database per test file and drop it afterwards, so a long-lived emulator grows until it runs out of memory. With a production-like schema (about 220 migrations, 855 DDL statements), an emulator container with a 2 GiB memory limit was OOM-killed on the fifth create/migrate/drop cycle. Clients talking to it then hang until their own timeouts.

Possibly related: #277, which reports suites slowing down across runs against one long-lived emulator that creates a database per test.

### Expected

After a database is dropped, the emulator's memory returns to roughly its level before that database was created.

### Repro script (`repro.mjs`)

```js
// Minimal, self-contained repro for two Cloud Spanner emulator behaviours:
//  1. Memory used by a database is not released after the database is dropped.
//  2. (On linux/amd64) v1.5.58 applies a large schema noticeably slower than v1.5.57.
//
// It repeatedly creates a database, applies a synthetic schema through many
// small schema changes (like a migration history), and drops the database.
//
//   npm install @google-cloud/spanner
//   docker run -d --name emu -p 9010:9010 gcr.io/cloud-spanner-emulator/emulator:<version>
//   SPANNER_EMULATOR_HOST=localhost:9010 EMULATOR_CONTAINER=emu node repro.mjs
//
// EMULATOR_CONTAINER is optional; when set, container memory is read with `docker stats`.
// DATA=1 adds one insert and one query per database (opens client sessions);
// CLOSE=1 closes the database handle before dropping it. Neither changes the result.
import { execFileSync } from "node:child_process";
import { createRequire } from "node:module";

const require = createRequire(process.env.SPANNER_MODULE_FROM ?? import.meta.url);
const { Spanner } = require("@google-cloud/spanner");

const ITERATIONS = Number(process.env.ITERATIONS ?? 8);
const TABLES = Number(process.env.TABLES ?? 260);
const container = process.env.EMULATOR_CONTAINER;

// ~480 schema-change calls, ~960 statements: one root table, TABLES-1 tables
// interleaved in it, one secondary index per table (a mix of plain, UNIQUE and
// NULL_FILTERED), and later ALTER TABLE ... ADD COLUMN changes to earlier tables.
function schemaChanges() {
  const changes = [["CREATE TABLE tenants (tenantId STRING(36) NOT NULL, name STRING(MAX), createdAt TIMESTAMP NOT NULL OPTIONS (allow_commit_timestamp=true)) PRIMARY KEY (tenantId)"]];
  let added = 0;
  for (let i = 1; i < TABLES; i++) {
    const t = `t${i}`;
    changes.push([
      `CREATE TABLE ${t} (
        tenantId STRING(36) NOT NULL,
        id STRING(36) NOT NULL,
        label STRING(MAX),
        status STRING(32) NOT NULL,
        amount INT64,
        enabled BOOL,
        effectiveOn DATE,
        payload JSON,
        blob BYTES(MAX),
        updatedAt TIMESTAMP NOT NULL OPTIONS (allow_commit_timestamp=true),
      ) PRIMARY KEY (tenantId, id), INTERLEAVE IN PARENT tenants ON DELETE CASCADE`,
      i % 3 === 0
        ? `CREATE UNIQUE NULL_FILTERED INDEX idx_${t}_label ON ${t}(tenantId, label)`
        : i % 3 === 1
          ? `CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_${t}_status ON ${t}(tenantId, status, id)`
          : `CREATE INDEX idx_${t}_updated ON ${t}(tenantId, updatedAt DESC) STORING (status)`,
    ]);
    // After the first 40 tables, every new table brings ALTERs to two earlier ones.
    if (i > 40) {
      const alters = [];
      for (const target of [i - 40, i - 20]) {
        added += 1;
        alters.push(`ALTER TABLE t${target} ADD COLUMN extra${added} STRING(MAX)`);
      }
      changes.push(alters);
    }
  }
  return changes;
}

const memMiB = () => {
  if (!container) return null;
  const used = execFileSync("docker", ["stats", "--no-stream", "--format", "{{.MemUsage}}", container]).toString().split("/")[0].trim();
  const n = parseFloat(used);
  return Math.round(used.includes("GiB") ? n * 1024 : used.includes("KiB") ? n / 1024 : n);
};

const spanner = new Spanner({ projectId: "repro-project" });
const instance = spanner.instance("repro-instance");
const [exists] = await instance.exists();
if (!exists) {
  const [, op] = await instance.create({ config: "emulator-config", nodes: 1, displayName: "repro" });
  await op.promise();
}
const changes = schemaChanges();
console.log(JSON.stringify({ schemaChanges: changes.length, statements: changes.flat().length, memMiB: memMiB() }));
for (let n = 1; n <= ITERATIONS; n++) {
  const db = instance.database(`repro-${Date.now().toString(36)}-${n}`);
  const started = performance.now();
  const [, created] = await instance.createDatabase(db.id);
  await created.promise();
  for (const ddl of changes) {
    const [op] = await db.updateSchema(ddl);
    await op.promise();
  }
  const buildMs = Math.round(performance.now() - started);
  if (process.env.DATA === "1") {
    await db.table("tenants").insert([{ tenantId: "tenant-1", name: "Tenant", createdAt: Spanner.COMMIT_TIMESTAMP }]);
    await db.run({ sql: "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM tenants" });
  }
  if (process.env.CLOSE === "1") await db.close();
  await db.delete();
  console.log(JSON.stringify({ iteration: n, buildMs, memMiBAfterDrop: memMiB() }));
}
spanner.close();
```

`EmulatorFeatureFlags` is documented as thread-safe, and its `flags_` member is
annotated with `ABSL_GUARDED_BY(mu_)`. However, `flags()` returns a reference to
`flags_` after its local reader lock has been released:

```cpp
const Flags& flags() const ABSL_LOCKS_EXCLUDED(mu_) {
  absl::ReaderMutexLock l(&mu_);
  return flags_;
}
```

The lock is destroyed when `flags()` returns, before the caller reads through
the returned reference. A concurrent `set_flags()` call can therefore modify
the same object while that reference is being read.

I reproduced this with both Clang Thread Safety Analysis and ThreadSanitizer at
the current `master` revision,
`2abe04c1ace40b8760133e6ca0800d257d127da4`.

### Static diagnostic

Building the real `//backend/locking:manager` target with Clang 18 thread-safety
analysis reports one project-owned reference-return diagnostic:

```text
./common/feature_flags.h:68:12: warning: returning variable 'flags_' by
reference requires holding mutex 'mu_' [-Wthread-safety-reference-return]
   68 |     return flags_;
      |            ^
```

The relevant build invocation was equivalent to:

```shell
CC=/usr/bin/clang-18 CXX=/usr/bin/clang++-18 \
  bazel build //backend/locking:manager \
  --check_direct_dependencies=off \
  --action_env=CC=/usr/bin/clang-18 \
  --action_env=CXX=/usr/bin/clang++-18 \
  --repo_env=CC=/usr/bin/clang-18 \
  --repo_env=CXX=/usr/bin/clang++-18 \
  --copt=-Wthread-safety
```

The issue is the lifetime relationship rather than a missing lock acquisition:
the getter does acquire `mu_`, but the returned alias outlives that lock.

### ThreadSanitizer reproduction

I added the following temporary probe. It is only a reproducer, not part of the
proposed project change.

<details>
<summary><code>common/feature_flags_tsan_probe.cc</code></summary>

```cpp
#include "common/feature_flags.h"

#include <atomic>
#include <cstdint>
#include <thread>

namespace emulator = ::google::spanner::emulator;

int main() {
  auto& mutable_flags = const_cast<emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags&>(
      emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::instance());
  std::atomic<bool> start = false;
  std::atomic<bool> stop = false;
  std::atomic<std::uint64_t> observations = 0;

  std::thread writer([&] {
    while (!start.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
    }
    emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::Flags flags;
    for (int i = 0; i < 500000; ++i) {
      const bool value = (i & 1) != 0;
      flags.enable_check_constraint = value;
      flags.enable_column_default_values = value;
      flags.enable_protos = value;
      flags.enable_dml_returning = value;
      mutable_flags.set_flags(flags);
    }
    stop.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
  });

  std::thread reader([&] {
    start.store(true, std::memory_order_release);
    std::uint64_t local = 0;
    while (!stop.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) {
      const auto& flags = emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::instance().flags();
      local += flags.enable_check_constraint;
      local += flags.enable_column_default_values;
      local += flags.enable_protos;
      local += flags.enable_dml_returning;
    }
    observations.store(local, std::memory_order_relaxed);
  });

  writer.join();
  reader.join();
  return observations.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) == UINT64_MAX;
}
```

</details>

The corresponding temporary Bazel target is:

```starlark
load("@rules_cc//cc:cc_binary.bzl", "cc_binary")

cc_binary(
    name = "feature_flags_tsan_probe",
    srcs = ["feature_flags_tsan_probe.cc"],
    linkopts = ["-pthread"],
    deps = [":feature_flags"],
)
```

I built and ran it with:

```shell
bazel build //common:feature_flags_tsan_probe \
  --check_direct_dependencies=off \
  --copt=-O1 --copt=-g \
  --copt=-fsanitize=thread \
  --linkopt=-fsanitize=thread

TSAN_OPTIONS=halt_on_error=1:exitcode=66 \
  ./bazel-bin/common/feature_flags_tsan_probe
```

The mutation path mirrors the existing
`ScopedEmulatorFeatureFlagsSetter`, which also obtains the singleton through
`const_cast` and invokes `set_flags()`.

With the current getter, the probe exits with the configured TSAN failure code
(`66`) and reports:

```text
WARNING: ThreadSanitizer: data race
  Write of size 1 ... by thread T1 (mutexes: write M0)
  Previous read of size 1 ... by thread T2
  Location is heap block ... allocated by
  google::spanner::emulator::EmulatorFeatureFlags::instance()
```

### Proposed repair

Returning a snapshot by value keeps the copy inside the reader-lock scope:

```diff
-  const Flags& flags() const ABSL_LOCKS_EXCLUDED(mu_) {
+  Flags flags() const ABSL_LOCKS_EXCLUDED(mu_) {
     absl::ReaderMutexLock l(&mu_);
     return flags_;
   }
```

After this one-line change:

- the same TSAN probe exits `0` with no data-race report; and
- a focused Clang 18 build passes with both `-Wthread-safety` and
  `-Werror=thread-safety-reference-return`.

The current call-site forms that immediately inspect one field, or initialize a
local `Flags` value, remain source-compatible and instead receive a consistent
snapshot.

### Impact and validation boundary

This demonstrates an API-level concurrency correctness problem in a class that
explicitly promises thread safety. I have not established that a production
emulator workload mutates these flags concurrently. In the current repository,
`set_flags()` appears to be used primarily through test support, so I do not
want to overstate the observed production impact.


Would returning `Flags` by value be an acceptable fix here, or is there a
preferred accessor pattern for this singleton?

In our team we started using the new [INSERT with ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING clause](https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reference/standard-sql/dml-syntax#insert_with_on_conflict_do_nothing_examples) which works in our cloud spanner instances. Whereas when we attempted to use it in our tests and local development we observed following error. 

```SQL Error [12]: UNIMPLEMENTED: com.google.api.gax.rpc.UnimplementedException: io.grpc.StatusRuntimeException: UNIMPLEMENTED: ON CONFLICT clause with empty conflict target in INSERT statement is not supported in Emulator```

Looks like the `ON CONFLICT` clause is not supported in emulator.

Sample DB schema and INSERT DMLs
```sql
CREATE TABLE test ( id INT64 NOT NULL, created_on TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP())) PRIMARY KEY (id);

INSERT INTO test (id, created_on) VALUES (1, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP());

INSERT INTO test (id, created_on) VALUES (1, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
```

Dialect - GoogleSQL

There seems to be a fundamental difference in how the production spanner service and the spanner emulator normalizes whitespace within the `SNIPPET` function.

Production Spanner normalizes text by collapsing multiple consecutive spaces and newlines into a single space, recalculating the begin and end offsets relative to this normalized snippet string. The emulator, however, leaves the original whitespace and newlines completely intact, causing both the raw text and the highlight position offsets to mismatch.

```sql
SELECT SNIPPET("test 123\nabc", "123")
```
Returns this from production

```json
{
  "snippets": [
    {
      "highlights": [{"begin": 6, "end": 9}],
      "snippet": "test 123 abc",
      "source_begin": 1,
      "source_end": 13
    }
  ]
}
```
and this from emulator (ignore the schema difference due to https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/cloud-spanner-emulator/issues/355)

```json
{
  "snippets": [
    {
      "highlights": [{"start_position": 6, "end_position": 9}],
      "snippet": "test 123\nabc"
    }
  ]
}
```

Same offsets.

But with this query:
```sql
SELECT SNIPPET("test       123\n abc", "123")
```
Production returns
```json
{
  "snippets": [
    {
      "highlights": [{"begin": 6, "end": 9}],
      "snippet": "test 123 abc",
      "source_begin": 1,
      "source_end": 20
    }
  ]
}
```
Notice the highlight offsets are the same and the snippet spaces are collapsed.

Emulator returns

```json
{
  "snippets": [
    {
      "highlights": [{"start_position": 12, "end_position": 15}],
      "snippet": "test       123\n abc"
    }
  ]
}
```

Where the higlight offsets and snippet text is keeping the whitespace intact.

Running Spanner Emulator Version: 1.5.54

When using the fts `SNIPPET` function the emulator's output schema diverges from what production spanner produces.

Specifically, the emulator Returns `start_position` and `end_position` as keys inside the highlights array (https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/cloud-spanner-emulator/blob/master/backend/query/search/snippet_evaluator.cc#L59-L60), whereas production returns `begin` and `end`. 

And it entirely omits the `source_begin` and `source_end` keys at the snippet level (https://github.com/GoogleCloudPlatform/cloud-spanner-emulator/blob/master/backend/query/search/snippet_evaluator.cc#L202)

Given a query like
```sql
SELECT
  SNIPPET(content, "test"),
FROM
  test_table
WHERE
  SEARCH(content_tokens, "test")
``` 

Production spanner returns 

```json
{
   "snippets":[
      {
         "highlights":[
            {
               "begin":1,
               "end":5
            }
         ],
         "snippet":"Test est",
         "source_begin":1,
         "source_end":9
      }
   ]
}
```
This matches what is documented here https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/reference/standard-sql/search_functions#snippet

The same query against the emulator returns
```json
{
   "snippets":[
      {
         "highlights":[
            {
               "start_position":1,
               "end_position":5
            }
         ],
         "snippet":"Test est"
      }
   ]
}
```

Running Spanner Emulator Version: 1.5.54

Because of this discrepancy, application code and deserializers that work in production fail during local testing against the emulator unless environment-specific workarounds are added.

It would be nice if this could be fixed so the emulator returns the snippets using the same schema as production spanner.

I have the following protobuf schema:
```proto
package project.service;

message Parent {
  Child child = 1;
}

message Child {
  google.protobuf.Timestamp created_at = 1;
}
```
And the following DDL:
```sql
CREATE TABLE my_table(
    parent      project.service.Parent NOT NULL,
    id          STRING(MAX) NOT NULL,
    created_at  TIMESTAMP NOT NULL AS (
        TIMESTAMP_ADD(
            TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(parent.child.created_at.seconds),
            INTERVAL parent.child.created_at.nanos NANOSECOND
        )
    ) STORED
) PRIMARY KEY (id);
```

And I have proto bundles created for `Parent`, `Child`, and `google.protobuf.Timestamp`.

I'm seeing that the DDL runs without error, but an error arises when inserting into the table (`readWriteTransaction`). It seems to be an issue with the `created_at` generated column. If it's simplified to, say,
```sql
created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL AS (TIMESTAMP_SECONDS(parent.child.created_at.seconds)) STORED
```
then there is no error, but this is an adequate precision.

There error message is quite vague: `UNKNOWN: io.grpc.StatusRuntimeException: UNKNOWN: Unexpected error in RPC handling`.

[Using latest image (1.5.51, f450d0c2a7316)
](https://console.cloud.google.com/artifacts/docker/cloud-spanner-emulator/us/gcr.io/emulator/sha256:f450d0c2a73164ed9bbda1f3b675931f133a78bf046314db86268cbeecc67fb6)

When a table uses `UUID`-typed columns as primary keys and a Change Stream is configured on that table, the `DataChangeRecord` `mods` entries contain `null` values for every UUID-typed key in the `keys` JSON. Non-key UUID columns in `new_values` / `old_values` are also affected since they go through the same code path.

This does **not** happen with `STRING(36)` columns. Changing the column type from `UUID` to `STRING(36)` causes the keys to appear correctly, confirming the issue is specific to the `UUID` type.

## Reproduction

1. Create a table with a `UUID` primary key and a change stream watching it:

```sql
CREATE TABLE Items (
    ItemId UUID NOT NULL,
    Name   STRING(256) NOT NULL,
) PRIMARY KEY (ItemId);

CREATE CHANGE STREAM ItemsStream
  FOR Items
  OPTIONS (
    retention_period = '7d',
    value_capture_type = 'NEW_ROW_AND_OLD_VALUES'
  );
```

2. Insert a row:

```sql
INSERT INTO Items (ItemId, Name) VALUES ('a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890', 'test');
```

3. Read the change stream using the `READ_ItemsStream` TVF.

4. Inspect the `mods[0].keys` JSON in the returned `DataChangeRecord`.

**Expected:**
```json
{"ItemId": "a1b2c3d4-e5f6-7890-abcd-ef1234567890"}
```

**Actual:**
```json
{"ItemId": null}
```

## Root cause

The `CloudValueToJSONValue` function in `backend/actions/change_stream.cc` handles the conversion of Spanner column values into JSON for change stream records. It has a `switch` on `zetasql::TypeKind` with cases for `TYPE_STRING`, `TYPE_INT64`, `TYPE_BYTES`, `TYPE_JSON`, `TYPE_EXTENDED`, etc., but is missing a case for `TYPE_UUID`. UUID values fall through to the `default` branch, which is a no-op, leaving the JSON reference unset (i.e., `null`).

## Fix

I don't have the current bandwidth to put up a PR (and am unable to sign the CLA), so here's what I did to fix this:

Adding a `TYPE_UUID` case that formats the value as a string (consistent with how `TYPE_JSON`, `TYPE_NUMERIC`, etc. are handled) resolves the issue:

```diff
diff --git a/backend/actions/change_stream.cc b/backend/actions/change_stream.cc
index 9979db45..e5bad99d 100644
--- a/backend/actions/change_stream.cc
+++ b/backend/actions/change_stream.cc
@@ -593,6 +593,10 @@ void CloudValueToJSONValue(const zetasql::Value value, JSONValueRef& ref) {
       ref.SetString(value.Format(false));
       break;
     }
+    case zetasql::TYPE_UUID: {
+      ref.SetString(value.Format(false));
+      break;
+    }
     default: {
       break;
     }
```

I built the emulator locally from source (v1.5.51, commit `50289aa9`) with this fix applied and confirmed that UUID-typed primary keys now correctly appear in change stream `keys` JSON, as well as in `new_values` and `old_values`.

Attempting to use "ON CONFLICT" with a table in a schema results in a "Table not found" error.

```
=> create schema temp_schema;
CREATE

=> create table temp_schema.widget(widget_id int not null primary key, name text not null);
CREATE

=> insert into temp_schema.widget(widget_id, name) values (1, 'name 1') on conflict (widget_id) do update set name = EXCLUDED.name RETURNING name;
ERROR:  Table not found: widget - Statement: 'insert into temp_schema.widget(widget_id, name) values (1, 'name 1') on conflict (widget_id) do update set name = EXCLUDED.name RETURNING name'

=> insert into temp_schema.widget(widget_id, name) values (1, 'name 1');
INSERT 0 1

=> insert into temp_schema.widget(widget_id, name) values (1, 'name 2') on conflict (widget_id) do update set name = EXCLUDED.name RETURNING name;
ERROR:  Table not found: widget - Statement: 'insert into temp_schema.widget(widget_id, name) values (1, 'name 2') on conflict (widget_id) do update set name = EXCLUDED.name RETURNING name'

=> insert into temp_schema.widget(widget_id, name) values (1, 'name 2') on conflict (widget_id) do nothing;
ERROR:  Table not found: widget
```

Works in <=1.5.43

When inserting a duplicate value that violates a UNIQUE INDEX constraint, the Spanner Emulator takes approximately 7 seconds to detect and return the error. This delay is significantly longer than expected and may impact development and testing workflows.

### Environment
- **Spanner Emulator**: Running on localhost:9010
- **@google-cloud/spanner**: ^7.0.0

### Steps to Reproduce

1. Create a table with a UNIQUE INDEX on a column (e.g., `email`)
2. Insert a record successfully
3. Attempt to insert another record with the same value for the unique column
4. Observe the delay before the error is returned

### Expected Behavior

The UNIQUE constraint violation should be detected and an error should be returned immediately (within milliseconds).

### Actual Behavior

The error is returned after approximately 7 seconds, which suggests the emulator may be performing unnecessary operations or timeouts before detecting the constraint violation.

### Code to Reproduce

```typescript
import { Spanner } from "@google-cloud/spanner";
import { randomUUID } from "crypto";

const ALREADY_EXISTS = 6;
const projectId = "dev-project";
const instanceId = "dev-instance";
const databaseId = "dev-database";
process.env.SPANNER_EMULATOR_HOST = "localhost:9010";

function log(...args: any[]): void {
  const timestamp = new Date().toISOString();
  console.log(`[${timestamp}]`, ...args);
}

const spanner = new Spanner({
  projectId: projectId,
});

export const db = spanner.instance(instanceId).database(databaseId);

interface User {
  id: string;
  name: string;
  email: string;
}

async function createUser(user: User): Promise<User> {
  try {
    return await db.runTransactionAsync(async (tx) => {
      const [rows] = await tx.run({
        sql: `INSERT users (id, name, email)
              VALUES (@id, @name, @email)
              THEN RETURN *`,
        params: {
          id: user.id,
          name: user.name,
          email: user.email
        },
      });

      await tx.commit();
      return rows[0].toJSON() as User;
    });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error && typeof error === "object" && "code" in error) {
      const grpcCode = (error as { code: number }).code;

      if (grpcCode === ALREADY_EXISTS) {
        throw new Error(`User with this email already exists: ${user.email}`);
      }
    }

    throw new Error(`Failed to create user: ${error}`);
  }
}

async function main() {
  const testEmail = "test@example.com";

  try {
    log("Attempting to create first user...");
    await createUser({
      id: randomUUID(),
      name: "Test User 1",
      email: testEmail,
    });
    log("✓ Successfully created first user");

    log("\nAttempting to create second user (same email)...");
    await createUser({
      id: randomUUID(),
      name: "Test User 2",
      email: testEmail, // same email
    });
    log("✗ No error occurred (unexpected behavior)");
  } catch (error: any) {
    log("Error message:", error.message);
    if (error && typeof error === "object" && "code" in error) {
      log("Error code:", (error as { code: number }).code);
      if ((error as { code: number }).code === ALREADY_EXISTS) {
        log("→ UNIQUE INDEX violation: User with this email already exists");
      }
    }
    if (error.details) {
      log("Error details:", JSON.stringify(error.details, null, 2));
    }
  }
}

main()
```

### Database Schema

```sql
CREATE TABLE users (
  id STRING(36) NOT NULL,
  name STRING(100) NOT NULL,
  email STRING(255) NOT NULL
) PRIMARY KEY(id);

CREATE UNIQUE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email);
```

Hello all,

Thanks for the continued support of the Spanner emulator. We use the emulator in our test suite fairly extensively.

Our tests are set up to use a shared emulator instance for all tests, and distinct databases are created for the life of each test. Some of the databases have a high number of tables (~350) and we observe creating them for each test is slow.

We instrumented the emulator and noticed there might be an opportunity to optimize `SchemaGraphEditor::IsOriginalNode` by using a Set for lookups instead of traversing the nodes: 

